Quantum Metric / FC360
CyberArk Privilege Cloud Integration | Solution Architecture & Detailed Design
Version 1.0  |  Design status: Proposed — subject to proof of concept and security approval
EXECUTIVE DECISION  •  Use a vaulted Quantum Metric local account, custom CPM password rotation, PSM web connection automation, and a separately vaulted static private decryption key. Investigators connect through native RDP; after automated sign-in and key submission, the live recorded session remains available. Approved local-drive redirection enables file export where permitted.
1. Executive summary
This design provides investigators with controlled, recorded access to the Quantum Metric web application supporting FC360 investigations. Individual investigators authenticate to CyberArk Privilege Cloud; a shared Quantum Metric local account is brokered by CyberArk PSM. A custom PSM WebApp connection component signs in and enters a separately protected text-format private decryption key. PSM then hands over the authenticated browser without revealing the vaulted account password through the normal Connect workflow. A dedicated CPM plugin changes the shared local-account password, while the long-lived decryption key remains unchanged.
Key design constraint: the existing Quantum Metric private decryption key must not be rotated or overwritten without a proven historical-data recovery/migration strategy. Neither a generic web connector nor PSM recording alone proves the private key is inaccessible to the investigator after injection.
2. Objectives, scope and non-goals
2.1 In scope
•	Vault and manage a shared Quantum Metric local user account; rotate its password with CPM after target workflow validation.
•	Store the private decryption key as a separate CyberArk static secret with tightly controlled administrative access.
•	Launch Quantum Metric from PSM through native RDP; automate credential login, key entry, Save, and successful-state checks.
•	Maintain the same interactive browser and continuous PSM session recording after automation ends.
•	Allow authorized evidence export using native RDP client-drive redirection, subject to security policy and PSM/browser tests.
•	Correlate CyberArk connection and retrieval events with Quantum Metric Privacy Audit and investigative case records.
2.2 Out of scope
HTML5 Gateway, browser-based SWS monitoring, automated investigation-file download, storing credentials in FC360 source code, key rotation, and key-decryption services/APIs are not required for this design.
3. Architecture and trust boundaries
Zone / actor	Component	Security responsibility
User endpoint	Managed Windows workstation + native mstsc	Individual login, approved .rdp launch, endpoint compliance, redirected drive
CyberArk control plane	Privilege Cloud / Safe / platform / permissions	Account and key custody, access authorization, audit, rotation policy
Session zone	PSM Windows host + hardened Edge + WebApp dispatcher	Recorded session, browser-based authentication and approved key submission
Target SaaS	Quantum Metric tenant / local account	Application authentication, private-key acceptance, privacy auditing
Operations	CPM or SRS execution environment	Verified shared-account password change; recovery under approved rights
Oversight	SIEM / case management	Attribution and alert correlation across platforms
3.1 Logical flow
[1] Investigator → CyberArk Privilege Cloud (individual login + MFA + authorized Connect)
[2] Privilege Cloud → Native .RDP launch → hardened PSM host
[3] PSM dispatcher ← vaulted Quantum Metric shared-account credential
[4] PSM browser → Quantum Metric login → authenticated dashboard
[5] PSM dispatcher ← separately vaulted static private key (approved linked-secret mechanism)
[6] Browser → Quantum Metric key input → Save → success verification
[7] Dispatcher initialization finishes; PSM window stays open → investigator interactive work
[8] Optional user-initiated download → PSM browser Save As → RDP redirected client drive
[9] PSM recording + Vault audit + Quantum Metric Privacy Audit → SIEM / case correlation
3.2 Security boundaries
The investigator receives an interactive browser, not direct Vault Retrieve permission. The CPM service requires separate change authority. The PSM session identity needs only the authorized secret retrieval mechanism, validated for the connector implementation. Because browser users may inspect controls or local browser state, key entry by automation is not equivalent to non-exportable key custody. The Quantum Metric product behavior must be assessed.
4. CyberArk object model
Object / configuration	Suggested name	Design note
Safe for target account	QM-FC360-PRD	Restricted app-account administration and PSM Connect
Safe for static private key	QM-FC360-KEY-PRD	Separate owner and recovery controls; no routine rotation
Quantum Metric account	QM-FC360-SharedUser	Username, target URL, custom CPM platform
Static key object	QM-FC360-PrivateKey	Text secret; preserve exact newlines and format
CPM platform	QM-Local-Web / QM-Local-API	Custom Verify/Change; Reconcile only if supported
Connection component	PSM-QuantumMetric	Browser login + post-login key Save + handoff
Access group	PAM-QM-Investigators	Connect and required List; no Retrieve/Change for regular users
Privileged key custodian	PAM-QM-Key-Custodians	Break-glass retrieval/update restricted and audited
4.1 Permissions and management
Assign investigators the minimum permissions necessary to see and initiate PSM connections. Avoid direct Retrieve Accounts, Update Account Content, Delete Accounts, and Safe administration. Ensure platform use permits PSM invocation. CPM service identity may need Get/Change privileges on the shared login credential. Key administrators should be independent of ordinary investigators; use dual control/approval where compatible with operational requirements.
5. CPM password management design
First choice: a supported Quantum Metric local-account password change API, if Quantum Metric confirms one exists. Second choice: the certified CyberArk Web Application CPM Plugin Framework, which automates browser-based password change. No dedicated Quantum Metric CPM plugin or publicly verified local-user rotation endpoint is established in this design.
Operation	Proposed sequence	Acceptance condition
Verify	Authenticate with vaulted current credential to a permitted endpoint	Success or deterministic authentication failure
Change	Authenticate → password settings → current/new password → Save	Re-login proves new password works; Vault commit correct
Reconcile	Privileged administrator/API sets target password independently	Only implement if officially supported and approved
Scheduling	Scheduled rotation with blackout/active-session rules	No unexpected investigator disruption
Failure handling	Diagnostics without logging passwords, retry/recovery procedure	No Vault/target desynchronization
Account concurrency and session-token invalidation after password change are Quantum Metric behaviors to validate; do not assume running investigations survive rotation.
6. PSM WebApp connector design
6.1 Runtime sequence
•	Launch a dedicated hardened Edge browser under the PSM session and navigate to the approved HTTPS tenant login URL.
•	Inject vaulted username/password into validated DOM fields; submit sign-in.
•	Wait for an unambiguous post-login element; handle loading delays, iframes and redirects.
•	Navigate through Quantum Metric privacy/key configuration to the text private-key input control.
•	Read the separate vaulted key through a supported linked-account or custom secure retrieval path; never hardcode it in WebFormFields or files.
•	Populate key text, preserving line breaks/special characters; click Save/Apply.
•	Validate that the key is accepted (prefer a non-sensitive success state rather than viewing decrypted customer information).
•	Exit initialization cleanly, leaving the browser process, investigator control and PSM recording active.
•	On failure: prevent unapproved handoff, provide generic error, preserve redacted diagnostics and close or terminate as defined in runbook.
6.2 Connector-specific engineering conditions
CyberArk WebFormFields can automate inputs, buttons and validations, but the actual Quantum Metric DOM selectors and application behavior are not yet known. The feasibility of using a linked secret token such as LogonAccount Password is conditional on platform configuration, supported credential properties, and exact framework syntax. This document intentionally does not present untested selectors or a production-ready parameter block.
Multiline PEM/private key text must be tested for formatting fidelity and acceptance by Quantum Metric. Validate supported maximum size, browser control type (textarea vs JavaScript editor), key masking, browser persistence, and whether Save requires additional prompts. If a standard WebApp dispatcher cannot perform required navigation or securely access the key, implement a vendor-approved custom dispatcher instead.
6.3 Recording and ownership
The CyberArk user is the investigator of record, despite Quantum Metric seeing a shared local account. PSM recording begins with session establishment and must remain enabled after automation completes. Quantum Metric Privacy Audit, when enabled, records decryption-specific events; a shared account may reduce its user-level attribution. Require a case ID or reason during investigations through a supported application/business process.
7. Native RDP and file transfer architecture
Use the standard native RDP workflow from Privilege Cloud; no HTML5 deployment is needed. CyberArk PSM supports drive mapping subject to connection-component settings and PSM/endpoint Group Policy. The investigator initiates a download; the file is saved by the browser running on PSM. Where an approved mapped client drive is visible to the browser Save As dialog, the investigator selects that redirected destination.
Illustrative redirected drive: \\tsclient\C   (actual visibility, paths and policy depend on mstsc and PSM configuration).
Setting / test	Design decision
Local drive mapping	Explicitly enable only for approved Quantum Metric users/component
GPO restrictions	Verify PSM host drive-redirection policies and relevant CyberArk hardening baselines
RDP client	User launches CyberArk-generated .rdp; local-drive resource must be permitted
Browser	Test Save As and standard downloads in hardened Edge
Destination	Approved managed-device drive/folder only; no personal endpoints
Transfer auditing	Record download action where available; supplement with DLP/endpoint and application logs
Temporary storage	Control PSM Downloads retention and clean session artifacts
Backflow risk	Mapped drives can expose bidirectional filesystem paths; assess DLP and malware controls
Drive redirection is supported generally but not automatically guaranteed within a custom WebApp connector. A practical end-to-end Save As/download test on your production-equivalent PSM/GPO/Edge baseline is a mandatory gate.
8. Key security and lifecycle
•	Never rotate the decryption private key under a standard CPM password policy.
•	Keep recovery escrow independently encrypted, protected and tested using non-production material.
•	Require documented break-glass custodian process for restoration or authorized maintenance.
•	Do not write key material to launcher commands, logs, screenshots, scripts, source code or ordinary temp files.
•	Assess whether entered key remains visible in DOM, stored in browser profile/localStorage, or accessible to the shared SaaS account.
•	Harden browser download, clipboard, developer tools and file access as appropriate, without breaking required investigative workflow.
•	Require Quantum Metric vendor confirmation for browser key persistence, concurrent sessions and whether historical key versioning can be supported.
9. Monitoring, audit and evidence
Event	Primary evidence	Correlation key
User granted Connect	CyberArk Safe/group permission history	User ID / change ticket
Investigator initiates PSM	CyberArk PSM connection audit and recording	Vault user + PSM session ID + time
Shared-account change	CPM event / platform logs	Account ID + time
Key-retrieval by connector	Vault audit where emitted / component telemetry	Account/key object ID + session time
Key submitted and accepted	Custom component redacted success/failure telemetry	PSM session ID + status
Customer data decrypted	Quantum Metric Privacy Audit	Shared account + session + reason + time
Evidence downloaded	Quantum Metric/download audit + endpoint controls	Session / filename metadata / case ID
PSM session recordings may contain sensitive personal information. Define retention, reviewer roles, export controls, and approval/access logging. Avoid logging or filming the literal private key when possible; any recorded appearance constitutes a sensitive artifact.
10. Implementation plan
Phase	Activities	Exit criteria
1. Discovery	Vendor Q&A; inspect Quantum Metric login, privacy UI, password change, audit, save/download	Approved workflow and known technical constraints
2. Vault build	Safes, groups, static-key object, login platform and permissions	No investigator direct key retrieval; recovery documented
3. CPM POC	API or Web CPM Verify/Change, optionally Reconcile	Repeatable tested rotation; failure recovery
4. PSM POC	Browser login, separate key entry, Save, validation, clean interactive handoff	Successful recorded native RDP session
5. RDP transfer	Map local drives, browser Save As, DLP and GPO testing	Files reach controlled endpoint, no undesired access
6. Assurance	Negative tests, concurrency, key exposure, SIEM, rollback, performance	Security sign-off and operations acceptance
7. Production	Change control, rollout, runbooks, monitoring	Operational acceptance and support handover
11. Test and acceptance matrix
ID	Test	Expected result
T01	Approved user initiates Connect	Native RDP opens only authorized PSM-QuantumMetric
T02	Unapproved user attempts Connect	Denied; audit event generated
T03	Login automation	Username/password injected and login verified
T04	Key injection and Save	Original text key accepted without corruption
T05	PSM handoff	Automation ends; browser interactive; recording continues
T06	Wrong/expired account password	No key injection; failure traced without secret logs
T07	Invalid key or missing Save control	Fails safely and reports generic error
T08	CPM password Change/Verify	Successful change and subsequent PSM login
T09	Two concurrent investigators	No key/session contamination; attribution retained
T10	Native RDP Save As	Controlled file downloadable to approved redirected drive
T11	Key exposure assessment	DOM, browser storage, recordings and logs reviewed
T12	Audit reconciliation	Case ↔ PSM user ↔ Quantum Metric decryption record
12. Risks and decisions
Risk / open decision	Treatment / owner
Shared Quantum Metric account removes native per-user attribution	Business owner + security approve; PSM/Privacy Audit correlation
Static decryption key cannot rotate safely	Key custodian: independent recovery and exposure response
Key observable after browser injection	Quantum Metric + security validate; compensate or redesign
Custom web selectors change following vendor upgrades	PAM team: regression tests and runbook
Quantum Metric local account may be SSO-only or password-change unsupported	Application owner/vendor: confirm account type and rotation
Linked key cannot be retrieved securely by stock dispatcher	PAM engineering: supported secure extension or custom dispatcher
Drive-mapping policy conflicts with hardened PSM browser	Windows/PSM team: controlled POC and GPO review
Shared account concurrent access/rekey behavior unknown	Application owner: load/session tests
13. Go / no-go decision gates
•	GO only after Quantum Metric confirms supported local-login/password-change and private-key-entry workflows.
•	GO only when the private key can be retrieved by the connector without granting investigator Vault retrieval permissions.
•	GO only when post-save browser handoff and recording work, and no key appears in operational logs.
•	GO only when native RDP file downloads work on hardened PSM under approved security controls.
•	GO only after historical-key preservation, independent recovery and risk acceptance are documented.
14. Operational procedures
14.1 Standard investigation
Investigator authenticates to Privilege Cloud, selects shared Quantum Metric account, clicks Connect using PSM-QuantumMetric, opens native RDP session, waits for browser and key-accepted validation, performs approved investigation and any permitted download, logs out, and closes PSM session. Capture case number and reason in the investigation system.
14.2 Rotation failure
Stop further scheduled attempts as appropriate, determine target/Vault sync state without displaying secret values, use approved recovery/reconcile path only if supported, then re-test PSM login. Never modify the separate decryption key as part of password recovery.
14.3 Key failure
Disable affected PSM connector workflow if key validation fails; verify unchanged key object and backup integrity via custodians; do not overwrite or regenerate the long-lived key in an incident response without business/crypto approval.
